<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mem and Zin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1692">1692</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mem û Zîn, Mem u Zin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mem and Zin is an epic poem in Kurmanji Kurdish written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani">Ahmad Khani</a> and completed in 1692. Based on a folk tale, it tells of the tragic love of Mem and Zin, a sister of the emir of Bohtan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a>, whose union is thwarted by the intrigues of the courtier Bakr. With its call for Kurdish unity, it is considered the classic work of Kurdish literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mem-and-zin.jpg" alt="Mem and Zin" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gomada · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gora_Mem_%C3%BB_Z%C3%AEn.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mem and Zin is the most important work of the Kurdish writer and poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani">Ahmad Khani</a> (1651–1707). He drew on a story handed down from generation to generation through oral tradition, of which his work is the best known of many versions. The oral narrative, known as Memê Alan, probably originated in the 14th century and was carried by the Dengbêj, the Kurdish singers of tales. In the 1930s the Kurdish prince Celadet Ali Bedirxan and the French orientalist Roger Lescot recorded the Memê Alan narrative with the help of several Dengbêj from Adiyaman and Afrin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and the full legend is now part of Kurdish literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Mem, a young man of the Alan clan and heir to the City of the West, falls in love with Zin of the Botan clan. Bakr of the Bakran clan, jealous of the lovers, blocks their union and in the end brings about Mem's death through a complicated conspiracy. Zin collapses and dies while mourning at his grave and is buried beside him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a>.</p><p>When Bakr's part is revealed, Tacdîn, Mem's best friend, kills him, and Bakr is buried beside the lovers, as Mem had asked so that his enemy might witness their love. A thorn bush fed by Bakr's blood grows from his grave, and its roots divide the two even in death. Alongside the love story the poem carries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> themes and a Kurdish national theme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1992 Ümit Elçi directed a film of Mem u Zin; because the Kurdish language was banned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> from 1980 until the late 1990s or early 2000s, it had to be released in Turkish. In 2002 Kurdistan TV produced a dramatised mini-series, Memi Alan, directed by Nasir Hassan. The Mem-u Zin Mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a> has become a place that visitors seek out.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mem and Zin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cizre was a centre of Kurdish learning: the poet Ahmad Khani set his epic <b>Mem u Zin</b> of 1692 at its court.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bohtan">Emirate of Bohtan</a></li></ul></section>
